WEBVTT

00:00:04.004 --> 00:00:05.232
Get out of the way!

00:00:07.640 --> 00:00:09.039
Oh.

00:00:09.109 --> 00:00:10.440
Whoa. Easy, now.

00:00:10.510 --> 00:00:12.307
Whoo.

00:00:15.548 --> 00:00:17.709
Hey, watch out!

00:00:17.784 --> 00:00:18.808
Come on. Come on.

00:00:18.885 --> 00:00:20.318
Sorry.

00:00:21.755 --> 00:00:22.983
Tommy, me boy...

00:00:27.560 --> 00:00:29.084
Did you hurt yourself,
Tommy-Boy?

00:00:30.530 --> 00:00:32.464
I think I zigged
when I should have zagged.

00:00:32.532 --> 00:00:35.592
Well, maybe you ought to stick
to a horse and carriage.

00:00:35.668 --> 00:00:36.965
Still, she's a beauty.

00:00:37.037 --> 00:00:38.971
The world must
be treating you well

00:00:39.105 --> 00:00:40.470
to afford such a thing.

00:00:41.541 --> 00:00:44.066
Just dipping
into my inheritance.

00:00:44.144 --> 00:00:46.578
Oh, come into some money,
did you?

00:00:46.646 --> 00:00:47.977
So who's passed on?

00:00:48.048 --> 00:00:51.074
Um, my grandfather
on my mother's side.

00:00:51.151 --> 00:00:53.745
An aristocrat,
God rest his soul.

00:00:53.820 --> 00:00:55.913
Sounds like he was a fine man.

00:00:55.989 --> 00:00:57.752
I'd drink a toast to him,

00:00:57.824 --> 00:00:59.951
if I had a shilling
to me name.

00:01:01.761 --> 00:01:04.457
Here. It's on me.

00:01:04.531 --> 00:01:08.023
It'll take three pints
to do a proper job, Tommy-Boy.

00:01:08.101 --> 00:01:10.365
And this isn't
even enough for two.

00:01:10.437 --> 00:01:12.200
There. That's all I've got.

00:01:12.272 --> 00:01:14.331
You're a generous man.

00:01:14.407 --> 00:01:15.635
Care to join me at Sullivan's?

00:01:15.708 --> 00:01:16.766
Ah, I wish I could,

00:01:16.843 --> 00:01:18.936
but I'm on my way
to Castle O'Dell.

00:01:19.012 --> 00:01:20.309
Night's coming.

00:01:20.380 --> 00:01:22.746
And they say
when the sun goes down,

00:01:22.816 --> 00:01:26.650
the queen of the faerie folk
reclaims the castle.

00:01:26.719 --> 00:01:29.119
Ah.

00:01:29.189 --> 00:01:30.781
I'll give her your regards.

00:01:30.857 --> 00:01:32.848
Don't say I didn't warn ya.

00:01:36.629 --> 00:01:40.963
Computer,
replace damaged tire.

00:01:43.636 --> 00:01:47.902
Saints preserve us.

00:03:39.452 --> 00:03:42.114
I thought I would die
from the shock of it.

00:03:42.188 --> 00:03:43.416
Now, Seamus,
you're not a great one

00:03:43.489 --> 00:03:45.218
for telling things
the way they were.

00:03:45.291 --> 00:03:46.451
I'll admit I've been known

00:03:46.526 --> 00:03:49.120
to add a little color
to stories from time to time

00:03:49.195 --> 00:03:52.653
to liven things up,
but with God as my witness

00:03:52.732 --> 00:03:55.166
I saw Tom Paris using
unnatural powers

00:03:55.235 --> 00:03:57.533
to fix that wheel of his.

00:03:57.604 --> 00:04:01.165
Tommy-Boy practicing
unholy magic?

00:04:01.241 --> 00:04:03.539
Next thing
you'll be telling me is

00:04:03.610 --> 00:04:05.578
you want to pay
for your own drinks.

00:04:06.946 --> 00:04:09.312
Make fun if you like,
but he was heading

00:04:09.382 --> 00:04:13.011
up to Castle O'Dell
only an hour before dark.

00:04:13.086 --> 00:04:14.917
Oh, leave it alone, Seamus.

00:04:14.988 --> 00:04:15.920
Can't one day go by

00:04:15.989 --> 00:04:17.513
without you making
an ass of yourself?

00:04:17.590 --> 00:04:18.352
Mm.

00:04:18.424 --> 00:04:20.324
But that Tom Paris
is an odd one.

00:04:20.393 --> 00:04:22.759
Coming and going through town
as if he built the place.

00:04:22.829 --> 00:04:25.320
That's right,
and where does he go?

00:04:25.398 --> 00:04:28.265
Oh, he's a shifty one,
and for that matter,

00:04:28.334 --> 00:04:29.426
so are his friends.

00:04:29.502 --> 00:04:30.901
If you're talking
about Katie O'Clare,

00:04:30.970 --> 00:04:32.062
you better watch your mouth.

00:04:32.138 --> 00:04:34.766
Oh. Well, you know what
they say, Sullivan...

00:04:34.841 --> 00:04:36.172
birds of a feather...

00:04:36.242 --> 00:04:40.872
They also say superstition
is the religion of fools.

00:04:40.947 --> 00:04:44.405
Kilmanin, 1846.

00:04:44.484 --> 00:04:45.542
What?

00:04:45.618 --> 00:04:47.677
Old Patsy down in County Meath

00:04:47.754 --> 00:04:52.691
spoke of a band of spirit folk
who came to Kilmanin in '46.

00:04:52.759 --> 00:04:54.158
Kilmanin?

00:04:54.227 --> 00:04:56.218
Did you ever hear of it, Doc?

00:04:56.296 --> 00:04:57.957
No, this is the first.

00:04:58.097 --> 00:05:00.793
Ah, but you won't
find it on any map.

00:05:00.867 --> 00:05:03.131
Not anymore.

00:05:03.202 --> 00:05:04.134
It was a town

00:05:04.203 --> 00:05:07.297
not much different
than Fair Haven.

00:05:07.373 --> 00:05:10.570
That is, until this group
of strangers arrived.

00:05:10.643 --> 00:05:14.204
The townsfolk welcomed them
with open arms.

00:05:14.280 --> 00:05:15.907
After all, a stranger

00:05:16.015 --> 00:05:18.916
is just a friend
you haven't met yet.

00:05:18.985 --> 00:05:22.113
But they regretted
their hospitality soon enough.

00:05:22.188 --> 00:05:24.349
The cows stopped giving milk.

00:05:24.424 --> 00:05:27.052
The potatoes grew
rotten in the ground.

00:05:27.126 --> 00:05:28.923
Then people started
to go missing.

00:05:28.995 --> 00:05:30.223
Oh, go on.

00:05:30.296 --> 00:05:31.320
It's true.

00:05:31.397 --> 00:05:33.262
What happened to them?

00:05:33.333 --> 00:05:34.527
They were taken...

00:05:34.600 --> 00:05:36.795
to the otherworld.

00:05:36.869 --> 00:05:38.837
When winter came

00:05:38.905 --> 00:05:44.502
and the nights grew longer,
the entire town vanished

00:05:44.577 --> 00:05:46.408
every post, every nail...

00:05:46.479 --> 00:05:51.917
poof... into thin air,
never to be seen again.

00:05:51.984 --> 00:05:53.349
Are you suggesting

00:05:53.419 --> 00:05:56.320
that Fair Haven's going
to go the way of Kilmanin

00:05:56.389 --> 00:05:58.289
just because we have
a few new folks in town?

00:05:58.358 --> 00:06:02.260
I'm suggesting you keep an eye
on Tom Paris and his cohorts.

00:06:02.328 --> 00:06:05.297
Not to mention
that lady friend of yours.

00:06:05.365 --> 00:06:08.129
So Katie's some kind of
goblin now, is that it?

00:06:08.201 --> 00:06:11.364
She's certainly cast
a spell on you.

00:06:11.437 --> 00:06:12.699
Well, if you ask me...

00:06:14.006 --> 00:06:15.337
Katie!

00:06:15.408 --> 00:06:16.340
Am I interrupting?

00:06:16.409 --> 00:06:17.535
Aw, just some nonsense.

00:06:17.610 --> 00:06:19.407
Seamus is up
to his old tricks again.

00:06:19.479 --> 00:06:20.605
Good day to you, Katie.

00:06:20.680 --> 00:06:21.806
Oh, it's good to see you.

00:06:21.881 --> 00:06:22.813
What'll it be?

00:06:22.882 --> 00:06:25.009
The usual, please.

00:06:25.084 --> 00:06:26.745
Well, one cup of tea
coming right up.

00:06:26.819 --> 00:06:29.310
Did you snatch any children
on the way into town, Katie?

00:06:29.389 --> 00:06:30.822
What?

00:06:30.890 --> 00:06:33.358
Never mind.

00:06:33.426 --> 00:06:34.484
I missed your smile.

00:06:34.560 --> 00:06:36.528
What have you been
doing with yourself?

00:06:36.596 --> 00:06:37.927
Nothing terribly exciting.

00:06:37.997 --> 00:06:39.589
Just helping my aunt
and uncle out on the farm.

00:06:39.665 --> 00:06:42.532
They're not as spry
as they used to be.

00:06:42.602 --> 00:06:44.069
Although, they've got
a goat that could argue

00:06:44.137 --> 00:06:46.697
Mossie Donegan's talking pig
under the table.

00:06:46.773 --> 00:06:47.933
You'll have to bring
him into the bar

00:06:48.040 --> 00:06:49.029
the next time you're up.

00:06:49.108 --> 00:06:50.632
We'll arrange a debate.

00:06:50.710 --> 00:06:51.870
I'll see that I do.

00:06:51.944 --> 00:06:54.412
I hear they opened
a nickelodeon in Dooleen.

00:06:54.480 --> 00:06:57.176
I thought it might
be nice to go to a show.

00:06:57.250 --> 00:06:58.239
I've got to tend the bar.

00:06:58.317 --> 00:06:59.716
I wish you'd let me
know you were coming.

00:06:59.786 --> 00:07:00.810
I would've made arrangements.

00:07:00.887 --> 00:07:02.411
That's all right.

00:07:02.488 --> 00:07:03.580
We can go some other time.

00:07:03.656 --> 00:07:04.987
No, I mean, you've
already made the trip.

00:07:05.057 --> 00:07:07.116
It'd be a shame
if you have to leave.

00:07:07.193 --> 00:07:09.252
Well, I could be
convinced to stay

00:07:09.328 --> 00:07:12.354
if you'd be up
for a game of... rings.

00:07:14.467 --> 00:07:15.525
Let's go.

00:07:20.807 --> 00:07:23.173
Computer, one dozen
Broadway lilies.

00:07:29.182 --> 00:07:31.673
Something tells me
that's not lunch.

00:07:31.751 --> 00:07:32.683
Already ate.

00:07:32.752 --> 00:07:34.151
Ah. Who are they for?

00:07:34.220 --> 00:07:35.482
A friend.

00:07:35.555 --> 00:07:38.547
Oh, and this friend
wouldn't happen to be

00:07:38.624 --> 00:07:40.922
a lovely Irish lass,
now, would she?

00:07:41.060 --> 00:07:42.789
Aren't you due on the Bridge?

00:07:42.862 --> 00:07:44.261
Ah, you better watch
yourself, Harry.

00:07:44.330 --> 00:07:45.922
These country girls aren't
as simple as you think.

00:07:46.032 --> 00:07:48.523
Maggie O'Halloran
could be a little more

00:07:48.601 --> 00:07:50.592
than you can handle.

00:07:50.670 --> 00:07:52.467
I heard about your
little traffic accident.

00:07:53.940 --> 00:07:55.567
That was a clutch malfunction.

00:07:55.641 --> 00:07:56.630
It wasn't my fault.

00:07:56.709 --> 00:07:58.199
If you ask me,
that whole program

00:07:58.277 --> 00:08:00.302
is an accident
waiting to happen.

00:08:00.379 --> 00:08:02.870
You have been running
Fair Haven around the clock.

00:08:02.982 --> 00:08:04.415
Just yesterday,
I had to replace

00:08:04.484 --> 00:08:05.815
three holo-emitters.

00:08:05.885 --> 00:08:08.217
I ran a full diagnostic
this morning.

00:08:08.287 --> 00:08:09.254
Everything is fine.

00:08:09.322 --> 00:08:12.485
Whatever you say.

00:08:12.558 --> 00:08:14.583
But when your quaint,
little seaside town

00:08:14.660 --> 00:08:16.753
starts to depolarize,
don't come running to me.

00:08:18.431 --> 00:08:22.060
If you'll excuse me,
I'm late for a moonlight stroll.

00:08:22.134 --> 00:08:23.499
Are you sure you
don't need a chaperon?

00:08:24.804 --> 00:08:26.271
Not with your driving record.

00:08:28.107 --> 00:08:30.905
I think I know just
what'll make his date

00:08:31.010 --> 00:08:34.002
just a little more interesting.

00:08:34.080 --> 00:08:38.414
Just don't blow out
any more holo-emitters.

00:09:00.039 --> 00:09:01.472
What are we doing out here,
Seamus,

00:09:01.541 --> 00:09:03.771
skulking about in the
dark, spying on folks?

00:09:03.843 --> 00:09:05.105
What's the matter, Milo?

00:09:05.177 --> 00:09:06.769
You got a case of the willies?

00:09:06.846 --> 00:09:08.279
Well, what if you're right?

00:09:08.347 --> 00:09:10.815
What if this Tom Paris
is some kind of demon?

00:09:10.883 --> 00:09:12.874
If we're caught, he'll
cast a spell on us.

00:09:12.952 --> 00:09:15.113
Then stop your squawking
or he'll catch us for sure.

00:09:15.187 --> 00:09:16.279
Come on, man.
He's getting away.

00:09:16.355 --> 00:09:17.549
Ah.

00:09:19.592 --> 00:09:22.618
A captain is only as good
as his crew.

00:09:22.695 --> 00:09:23.992
It must be lonely
out there on the sea

00:09:24.063 --> 00:09:26.896
with only the stars
to guide you.

00:09:27.033 --> 00:09:29.399
Sometimes,
during the night watch,

00:09:29.468 --> 00:09:31.902
with the timbers creaking,
and the dark water

00:09:32.004 --> 00:09:34.063
stretching out as far
as the eye can see,

00:09:34.140 --> 00:09:37.075
you can believe you're
the only man in the world.

00:09:37.143 --> 00:09:38.440
Maybe you should think

00:09:38.511 --> 00:09:40.376
about settling
down on dry land.

00:09:40.446 --> 00:09:41.708
Well, believe me, I do.

00:09:41.781 --> 00:09:43.442
As often as I think of you.

00:09:43.516 --> 00:09:45.916
Can I ask you something, Harry?

00:09:46.052 --> 00:09:46.814
Of course.

00:09:46.886 --> 00:09:50.185
Would you like to hold my hand?

00:09:50.256 --> 00:09:51.689
If you promise
to hold mine back.

00:09:57.930 --> 00:10:00.899
Look. He's got some sort
of contraption in his hand.

00:10:00.967 --> 00:10:03.435
Ooh, maybe we're going
about this the wrong way.

00:10:03.502 --> 00:10:05.697
We should go down to St. Mary's

00:10:05.771 --> 00:10:07.636
and tell Father Mulligan
the whole story.

00:10:07.707 --> 00:10:09.937
He's not going to listen
to talk of the supernatural.

00:10:10.042 --> 00:10:11.509
Aw, if you fill
his collection box,

00:10:11.577 --> 00:10:12.942
he'll listen to anything.

00:10:13.045 --> 00:10:14.376
That's true.

00:10:14.447 --> 00:10:16.608
I thought you were promised
to Ray Ewan.

00:10:16.682 --> 00:10:17.614
Not anymore.

00:10:17.683 --> 00:10:18.741
Ah.

00:10:18.818 --> 00:10:20.115
Can I ask you
something else, Harry?

00:10:20.186 --> 00:10:21.153
Please.

00:10:21.220 --> 00:10:23.381
Would you like to kiss me?

00:10:23.456 --> 00:10:25.117
Is that a trick question?

00:10:29.929 --> 00:10:32.227
Holy Mother of God!

00:10:34.433 --> 00:10:36.424
Tom!

00:10:39.872 --> 00:10:41.567
Don't you have
anything better to do?

00:10:41.641 --> 00:10:45.133
Oh, boy.
She really turned on you.

00:10:45.211 --> 00:10:46.303
Ha-ha, very funny.

00:10:46.379 --> 00:10:48.904
I could have been trampled.

00:10:48.981 --> 00:10:50.414
Relax. The holodeck
safeties are on.

00:10:50.483 --> 00:10:51.814
There's nothing to worry about.

00:10:51.884 --> 00:10:52.816
Poor Maggie.

00:10:52.885 --> 00:10:54.318
All right, all right.
You've had your fun.

00:10:54.387 --> 00:10:55.319
Now change her back.

00:10:55.388 --> 00:10:56.616
Chakotay to Paris and Kim.

00:10:56.689 --> 00:10:57.883
Report to the Bridge.

00:10:57.957 --> 00:10:59.219
On our way.

00:11:00.893 --> 00:11:01.882
What about Maggie?

00:11:01.961 --> 00:11:04.122
She'll be fine.
Let her graze.

00:11:04.196 --> 00:11:06.460
- Did you hear that?
- Ah!

00:11:11.704 --> 00:11:12.636
Amen.

00:11:12.705 --> 00:11:14.036
Please be seated.

00:11:21.647 --> 00:11:24.810
I recently heard a tale
of two farmers,

00:11:24.884 --> 00:11:28.320
who shall remain nameless
for the sake of this sermon.

00:11:28.387 --> 00:11:30.446
It seems one broke
the other's plow

00:11:30.523 --> 00:11:31.785
and refused to replace it.

00:11:31.857 --> 00:11:35.190
His comeuppance was served
to him not by the hand of God

00:11:35.261 --> 00:11:38.526
but by the other farmer,
in the form

00:11:38.597 --> 00:11:40.963
of a clogged irrigation ditch.

00:11:41.100 --> 00:11:43.694
Now, do you think
their disagreement

00:11:43.769 --> 00:11:45.566
stopped there?

00:11:45.638 --> 00:11:46.969
Of course not.

00:11:47.039 --> 00:11:49.837
They go on...

00:11:49.909 --> 00:11:52.673
just as the heathen did of old.

00:11:52.745 --> 00:11:53.871
After all, one spiteful act

00:11:53.946 --> 00:11:55.607
deserves another, right?

00:11:56.682 --> 00:11:57.706
Wrong!

00:11:57.783 --> 00:12:01.048
What these sinners
have forgotten

00:12:01.120 --> 00:12:03.384
is that they are neighbors.

00:12:03.456 --> 00:12:05.754
He's in love with the sound
of his own voice.

00:12:05.825 --> 00:12:07.656
Whether we are man or woman,

00:12:07.727 --> 00:12:09.058
parent or child...

00:12:09.128 --> 00:12:11.494
flesh and blood
or photons and force fields...

00:12:12.665 --> 00:12:14.633
It has been said...

00:12:14.700 --> 00:12:15.792
On the other hand,

00:12:15.868 --> 00:12:19.031
perhaps he's been nipping
at the sacramental wine.

00:12:19.105 --> 00:12:22.541
And, as such,
our community is everything.

00:12:22.608 --> 00:12:25.873
It is our world,
and we are a part of it

00:12:25.945 --> 00:12:29.813
just as a branch
is part of a tree.

00:12:29.882 --> 00:12:35.149
Do you see... branches
tearing leaves off one another?

00:12:35.221 --> 00:12:36.313
No!

00:12:36.388 --> 00:12:37.412
Do you see

00:12:37.490 --> 00:12:40.050
roots hoarding water
from the trunk?

00:12:40.126 --> 00:12:41.218
No!

00:12:41.293 --> 00:12:42.282
Do you see?

00:12:43.395 --> 00:12:44.327
Easy, Maggie.

00:12:44.396 --> 00:12:45.420
Whoa.

00:12:48.367 --> 00:12:49.459
Oh. Easy now.

00:12:50.536 --> 00:12:52.163
Did you make a wrong turn

00:12:52.238 --> 00:12:54.399
on your way to the milking shed?

00:12:54.473 --> 00:12:56.907
Oh, nothing like that
at all, Father.

00:12:56.976 --> 00:12:58.739
Something terrible's happened.

00:12:58.811 --> 00:13:00.802
What now?

00:13:00.880 --> 00:13:02.472
This was Maggie O'Halloran.

00:13:02.548 --> 00:13:06.040
The spirits
have turned her into a cow.

00:13:06.118 --> 00:13:09.485
Maggie O'Halloran's
been eaten by a cow?

00:13:09.555 --> 00:13:11.352
Did you see these spirits?

00:13:11.423 --> 00:13:12.788
That we did...

00:13:12.858 --> 00:13:15.349
Tom Paris and Harry Kim.

00:13:17.096 --> 00:13:19.530
I hate to disappoint you,
but it sounds as though

00:13:19.598 --> 00:13:22.032
you've been the victim
of a practical joke.

00:13:22.101 --> 00:13:23.898
Tom Paris is a known prankster.

00:13:23.969 --> 00:13:24.936
Oh...

00:13:25.004 --> 00:13:26.631
it was unholy magic, I tell you.

00:13:26.705 --> 00:13:27.672
Aye, aye.

00:13:27.740 --> 00:13:28.900
We saw it with our own eyes.

00:13:28.974 --> 00:13:33.673
When did this alleged
transformation take place?

00:13:33.746 --> 00:13:34.678
Last night.

00:13:34.747 --> 00:13:36.237
Well, then, that settles it.

00:13:36.315 --> 00:13:38.510
This couldn't possibly be
Maggie O'Halloran.

00:13:38.584 --> 00:13:40.950
I saw Maggie this morning
on my way into church.

00:13:41.053 --> 00:13:43.044
She was at her cart
tending her flowers.

00:13:43.122 --> 00:13:44.384
She's probably there now.

00:13:45.624 --> 00:13:46.591
Well, I... I think

00:13:46.659 --> 00:13:48.718
that's enough excitement
for one morning.

00:13:48.794 --> 00:13:49.726
This mass is over.

00:13:49.795 --> 00:13:51.729
Go in peace.

00:13:51.797 --> 00:13:53.264
Um... leave the cow.

00:13:53.332 --> 00:13:54.822
Oh...

00:13:54.900 --> 00:13:56.333
All right, Father.

00:13:56.402 --> 00:13:57.835
Excuse us, Maggie.

00:13:57.903 --> 00:13:59.234
Take care, Maggie.

00:14:03.809 --> 00:14:05.401
Whoa.

00:14:09.148 --> 00:14:10.740
Maggie...

00:14:10.816 --> 00:14:12.443
Well, good morning, boys.

00:14:12.518 --> 00:14:13.815
Is everything all right?

00:14:13.886 --> 00:14:16.787
Y-you're looking a little
out of sorts this morning.

00:14:16.856 --> 00:14:20.451
Yes. Well, I must admit
I'm not quite myself today.

00:14:20.526 --> 00:14:21.618
Oh?

00:14:21.694 --> 00:14:23.662
Well, this is going
to sound very odd,

00:14:23.729 --> 00:14:26.755
but I feel like I just woke up
from the strangest dream,

00:14:26.832 --> 00:14:28.766
and I don't remember going
to sleep.

00:14:28.834 --> 00:14:30.631
You're right,
that does sound odd.

00:14:30.703 --> 00:14:34.662
Do you remember anything
about last night?

00:14:34.740 --> 00:14:36.367
Well, I was out with Harry Kim.

00:14:36.442 --> 00:14:38.307
He chatted me up
at The Ox and Lamb,

00:14:38.377 --> 00:14:40.743
and we were off on a
walk before I knew it.

00:14:40.813 --> 00:14:42.337
What did you do on your walk?

00:14:42.414 --> 00:14:43.346
We talked.

00:14:43.415 --> 00:14:45.940
What kind of a girl
do you think I am?

00:14:46.018 --> 00:14:48.953
Now, Maggie,
we weren't implying anything.

00:14:49.088 --> 00:14:50.612
- No!
- Do you recall

00:14:50.689 --> 00:14:53.522
anything else
about the evening?

00:14:53.592 --> 00:14:57.289
Ah, did anything out of the, um,
ordinary happen?

00:14:57.363 --> 00:14:58.489
Anything at all?

00:14:58.564 --> 00:15:00.498
I don't remember.

00:15:00.566 --> 00:15:03.729
Well, tell us
about the dream, then.

00:15:03.802 --> 00:15:05.394
Oh, it was most unpleasant.

00:15:05.471 --> 00:15:06.904
I was walking around town

00:15:07.039 --> 00:15:09.473
with nothing
but a bell around my neck.

00:15:09.541 --> 00:15:11.771
Well, somehow,
I wound up in church.

00:15:11.844 --> 00:15:14.278
Everyone was staring at me.

00:15:14.346 --> 00:15:15.608
You were there, Seamus...

00:15:15.681 --> 00:15:17.114
And you, too, Milo.

00:15:18.183 --> 00:15:21.584
The next thing I knew,
I was tending my flowers

00:15:21.654 --> 00:15:23.588
just as you boys walked up.

00:15:23.656 --> 00:15:26.716
Well, that's quite a story,
Maggie.

00:15:26.792 --> 00:15:28.726
Can we get a quick one?

00:15:28.794 --> 00:15:30.421
Maybe just a wee taste.

00:15:34.934 --> 00:15:36.367
So, I said to young Harry,

00:15:36.435 --> 00:15:38.926
"I'm not one for rainy days
and gray skies."

00:15:39.071 --> 00:15:41.471
As soon as the words were
out of my mouth,

00:15:41.540 --> 00:15:44.031
he called up the spirits
to do his bidding.

00:15:44.109 --> 00:15:46.942
A second later,
there wasn't a cloud in the sky.

00:15:47.012 --> 00:15:49.503
I was up near Ballahick farm
with little Mary.

00:15:49.581 --> 00:15:51.173
She was playing near the well.

00:15:51.250 --> 00:15:53.548
Somehow, she lost her footing
and fell in.

00:15:53.619 --> 00:15:55.143
I was so frightened.

00:15:55.220 --> 00:15:56.414
I didn't know what to do.

00:15:56.488 --> 00:15:58.718
So, I ran to the Colbys'
to get some help...

00:15:58.791 --> 00:16:02.591
but... when we returned,
there was Mary in the meadow,

00:16:02.661 --> 00:16:04.652
talking with Katie O'Clare.

00:16:04.730 --> 00:16:06.994
There wasn't a scratch on her.

00:16:07.066 --> 00:16:08.897
Katie said
I must've been mistaken

00:16:09.001 --> 00:16:10.832
when I saw her fall.

00:16:10.903 --> 00:16:12.894
There was no mistake.

00:16:12.972 --> 00:16:15.167
I saw something strange, too.

00:16:15.240 --> 00:16:17.606
Well, I hesitated
to say anything

00:16:17.676 --> 00:16:19.576
for fear you'd think me mad.

00:16:19.645 --> 00:16:22.637
But after listening
to your stories today,

00:16:22.715 --> 00:16:24.910
I feel you've a right to know.

00:16:24.984 --> 00:16:25.916
Well?

00:16:25.985 --> 00:16:27.384
Speak up.

00:16:27.453 --> 00:16:30.911
Last Sunday, after his sermon,

00:16:30.990 --> 00:16:36.223
I saw Father Mulligan
vanish into thin air.

00:16:37.363 --> 00:16:39.058
He's one of them, right enough.

00:16:39.131 --> 00:16:41.861
Father Mulligan, Tommy-Boy,
Katie and the rest...

00:16:41.934 --> 00:16:43.060
they're spirit folk.

00:16:43.135 --> 00:16:44.693
Well, what about
that Neelix fellow

00:16:44.770 --> 00:16:45.862
who runs The Ox and Lamb?

00:16:45.938 --> 00:16:47.872
Well, if he doesn't
look like a leprechaun,

00:16:47.940 --> 00:16:48.929
I don't know who does.

00:16:49.008 --> 00:16:50.270
They're all probably

00:16:50.342 --> 00:16:52.276
up at Castle O'Dell,
conspiring against us.

00:16:52.344 --> 00:16:53.606
Now...

00:16:53.679 --> 00:16:55.044
that's enough.

00:16:55.114 --> 00:16:58.083
I can't deny there's been
some unusual goings-on.

00:16:58.150 --> 00:16:59.117
But what are you proposing...

00:16:59.184 --> 00:17:00.776
that we run Katie
and her friends out of town?

00:17:00.853 --> 00:17:02.980
Or maybe we should line them up
in front of a firing squad...

00:17:03.055 --> 00:17:04.386
is that what you want?

00:17:04.456 --> 00:17:06.981
These people have been
our neighbors.

00:17:07.059 --> 00:17:08.492
We've become friends.

00:17:08.560 --> 00:17:09.891
Let's not forget that.

00:17:09.962 --> 00:17:12.829
Well, we can't
just sit here and do nothing

00:17:12.898 --> 00:17:14.991
while they take over Fair Haven.

00:17:15.067 --> 00:17:17.035
I'm not saying we do nothing...

00:17:17.102 --> 00:17:20.902
but let's go about this
like civilized people.

00:17:20.973 --> 00:17:22.600
What do you suggest...

00:17:22.674 --> 00:17:25.939
we sit them down
and discuss it over tea?

00:17:42.828 --> 00:17:45.262
You're looking lovelier
than ever, Katie.

00:17:45.330 --> 00:17:46.297
Thank you.

00:17:50.669 --> 00:17:51.966
Open it.

00:17:57.276 --> 00:18:00.211
"The Faerie Queene
by Edmund Spenser."

00:18:00.279 --> 00:18:01.371
Thank you, Michael.

00:18:01.447 --> 00:18:03.745
It's the longest poem
in the English language,

00:18:03.816 --> 00:18:05.875
and the first
to use a nine-line stanza.

00:18:05.951 --> 00:18:08.146
I'll start reading it tonight.

00:18:08.220 --> 00:18:09.653
I love epic poetry.

00:18:09.721 --> 00:18:10.653
Really?

00:18:10.722 --> 00:18:12.883
I didn't know that.

00:18:14.293 --> 00:18:16.557
It seems as if there's
a lot of things

00:18:16.628 --> 00:18:18.061
I don't know about you.

00:18:19.131 --> 00:18:20.655
I thought you'd want to know

00:18:20.732 --> 00:18:22.893
there's been some talk
about you and your friends.

00:18:22.968 --> 00:18:26.529
Strange things have been
happening around town.

00:18:26.605 --> 00:18:28.835
Folk are saying you're to blame.

00:18:28.907 --> 00:18:31.467
What kind of strange things?

00:18:31.543 --> 00:18:34.034
People using unholy magic...

00:18:34.113 --> 00:18:36.445
vanishing into thin air...

00:18:36.515 --> 00:18:38.415
changing the weather.

00:18:38.484 --> 00:18:39.416
By any chance,

00:18:39.485 --> 00:18:41.817
would all of this
have anything to do

00:18:41.887 --> 00:18:43.912
with your reading suggestion?

00:18:44.056 --> 00:18:45.387
As a matter of fact.

00:18:45.457 --> 00:18:46.788
Don't tell me.

00:18:46.859 --> 00:18:49.828
They say
I'm the "Faerie Queene."

00:18:49.895 --> 00:18:52.693
Frankly, they could tell me
you're the devil himself,

00:18:52.764 --> 00:18:53.822
and I wouldn't care.

00:18:54.900 --> 00:18:56.697
I'll try
not to take that personally.

00:18:56.768 --> 00:18:59.737
The sad truth of the matter
is...

00:18:59.805 --> 00:19:02.239
I don't know who you are.

00:19:02.307 --> 00:19:04.537
What do you mean?

00:19:04.610 --> 00:19:07.408
You've been lying to me, Katie.

00:19:07.479 --> 00:19:10.346
I've been doing some checking
with friends of mine

00:19:10.415 --> 00:19:11.439
in County Clare.

00:19:11.517 --> 00:19:13.747
Seems nobody's heard
of you there.

00:19:13.819 --> 00:19:15.616
It's a big county.

00:19:15.687 --> 00:19:17.587
And I've got a lot of friends.

00:19:19.424 --> 00:19:21.892
You've been evasive
from the day we met.

00:19:22.027 --> 00:19:23.756
I was a fool not to see it.

00:19:23.829 --> 00:19:27.287
Where do you go
when you leave here?

00:19:27.366 --> 00:19:30.460
There've been times I wanted
to talk to you...

00:19:30.536 --> 00:19:31.901
tell you about my day,

00:19:32.037 --> 00:19:33.732
about a book I'd read...

00:19:33.805 --> 00:19:36.365
just to say hello...

00:19:36.441 --> 00:19:38.033
but I can't,

00:19:38.110 --> 00:19:40.578
because I don't know
where to find you.

00:19:42.114 --> 00:19:44.639
What are you hiding, Katie?

00:19:46.351 --> 00:19:49.548
I'll admit I have misled you
about some things,

00:19:49.621 --> 00:19:51.919
but never
about anything important.

00:19:52.024 --> 00:19:55.391
And I wasn't lying
when I said I care about you.

00:19:55.460 --> 00:19:57.928
I wish I could believe that.

00:19:58.063 --> 00:19:59.325
You can.

00:19:59.398 --> 00:20:02.162
I didn't want to deceive you...

00:20:02.234 --> 00:20:06.227
but under the circumstances,
I had no choice.

00:20:06.305 --> 00:20:08.296
What circumstances?

00:20:08.373 --> 00:20:09.635
Michael...

00:20:09.708 --> 00:20:11.938
there are things about me

00:20:12.044 --> 00:20:15.104
you may not be able
to understand.

00:20:15.180 --> 00:20:16.647
Try me.

00:20:19.184 --> 00:20:21.618
Who are you, Katie?

00:20:21.687 --> 00:20:24.622
Where are you really from?

00:20:24.690 --> 00:20:26.317
The truth.

00:20:37.202 --> 00:20:38.760
Computer, end program.

00:20:46.311 --> 00:20:47.278
How did he know?

00:20:47.346 --> 00:20:48.438
Those are questions

00:20:48.513 --> 00:20:50.276
no holodeck character
should be asking.

00:20:50.349 --> 00:20:51.782
What exactly did you
do to that program?

00:20:51.850 --> 00:20:54.580
Well, I, I just added
a few bells and whistles

00:20:54.653 --> 00:20:56.086
to make the characters
more realistic.

00:20:56.154 --> 00:20:57.086
Nothing too fancy.

00:20:57.155 --> 00:20:58.213
Well, something's gone wrong.

00:20:58.290 --> 00:20:59.416
The people of Fair Haven

00:20:59.491 --> 00:21:02.392
aren't simple country
folk anymore. Report.

00:21:02.461 --> 00:21:04.190
It looks like we've
worn out our welcome.

00:21:04.263 --> 00:21:06.026
It's the first time
we've tried running

00:21:06.098 --> 00:21:07.258
a holodeck program nonstop.

00:21:07.332 --> 00:21:08.799
We're lucky it lasted this long.

00:21:08.867 --> 00:21:11.836
There are damaged subroutines
in all of the character files.

00:21:11.903 --> 00:21:14.736
So much for my open door policy.

00:21:14.806 --> 00:21:17.639
Shut it down and repair
the damaged systems.

00:21:20.846 --> 00:21:22.780
As I recall, everything was fine

00:21:22.848 --> 00:21:25.783
until someone started
turning people into cows.

00:21:27.185 --> 00:21:31.053
Computer, display Fair Haven
character Michael Sullivan.

00:21:32.324 --> 00:21:33.586
Tom.

00:21:35.627 --> 00:21:36.924
Where am I?

00:21:36.995 --> 00:21:38.587
Where's Katie?

00:21:38.664 --> 00:21:40.723
He shouldn't be aware
that he's in the lab.

00:21:40.799 --> 00:21:42.630
I've isolated
the damaged subroutines.

00:21:42.701 --> 00:21:44.066
I said where the hell am I?!

00:21:44.136 --> 00:21:45.603
L-it's all right, Michael.

00:21:45.671 --> 00:21:46.603
Take it easy.

00:21:46.672 --> 00:21:47.639
What is this place?

00:21:47.706 --> 00:21:48.695
Try to relax.

00:21:48.774 --> 00:21:50.537
If someone doesn't tell me
where I am,

00:21:50.609 --> 00:21:52.201
I'm going to start
busting heads.

00:21:52.277 --> 00:21:53.767
His perceptual filters
are malfunctioning.

00:21:53.845 --> 00:21:55.779
What are you saying?
What's a perceptual filter?

00:21:55.847 --> 00:21:58.839
L-it's o... it's okay.
We're trying to help you.

00:21:58.917 --> 00:22:00.179
There.

00:22:00.252 --> 00:22:01.276
Look at these.

00:22:01.353 --> 00:22:02.911
Those are the algorithms

00:22:02.988 --> 00:22:04.922
designed to keep
him oblivious

00:22:05.057 --> 00:22:06.888
to anything outside
the program's parameters.

00:22:06.958 --> 00:22:07.925
They're off-line.

00:22:07.993 --> 00:22:09.688
Give me a minute.

00:22:10.862 --> 00:22:12.227
That should do it.

00:22:12.297 --> 00:22:13.730
How are you feeling, Michael?

00:22:16.168 --> 00:22:18.659
Never better.

00:22:18.737 --> 00:22:21.069
Harry, Liam's been looking
for you.

00:22:21.139 --> 00:22:22.606
You're the only man
in four counties

00:22:22.674 --> 00:22:23.868
to beat him at arm wrestling.

00:22:23.942 --> 00:22:25.375
He wants a rematch.

00:22:25.444 --> 00:22:26.741
Tell him he's got one.

00:22:26.812 --> 00:22:28.006
I'll do that.

00:22:29.348 --> 00:22:30.940
That was easy enough.

00:22:31.049 --> 00:22:31.947
Yeah. Unfortunately,

00:22:32.084 --> 00:22:33.915
it looks like
all the other characters

00:22:33.985 --> 00:22:35.282
have the same malfunction.

00:22:36.355 --> 00:22:37.379
Don't tell me we're going

00:22:37.456 --> 00:22:39.151
to have to go through
every one of them?

00:22:39.224 --> 00:22:41.658
If we reactivate the program,
use the primary control port

00:22:41.727 --> 00:22:43.661
in Sullivan's pub,

00:22:43.729 --> 00:22:45.959
we could reset all
the perceptual filters

00:22:46.031 --> 00:22:47.464
with a single command sequence.

00:22:47.532 --> 00:22:49.159
Ah. I guess we'll be
seeing you later.

00:22:49.234 --> 00:22:51.930
Looking forward to it.

00:22:52.003 --> 00:22:54.494
Computer, resume Fair Haven
program in Holodeck 1,

00:22:54.573 --> 00:22:57.098
and transfer Michael Sullivan
back to his bar.

00:23:10.922 --> 00:23:12.719
Danny!

00:23:12.791 --> 00:23:14.019
Danny, come here.

00:23:14.092 --> 00:23:15.650
Fetch Doc Fitzgerald.

00:23:15.727 --> 00:23:18.218
Tell him to meet me
in the church.

00:23:18.296 --> 00:23:19.661
Go on, hurry, lad.

00:23:23.668 --> 00:23:25.932
We thought we'd reset
his perceptual filters,

00:23:26.071 --> 00:23:28.562
until we took a closer look
at his subroutines.

00:23:28.640 --> 00:23:30.540
Turns out he was just
playing along.

00:23:30.609 --> 00:23:32.577
Ha. Never underestimate
an Irish hologram.

00:23:32.644 --> 00:23:35.477
We'll have to reprogram Michael
when we do the others.

00:23:35.547 --> 00:23:37.947
Let's just hope he
didn't share the experience

00:23:38.083 --> 00:23:39.209
with too many others.

00:23:39.284 --> 00:23:41.275
Got it.

00:23:41.353 --> 00:23:43.617
These resequenced algorithms
should do the trick.

00:23:43.688 --> 00:23:44.985
Good luck.

00:23:47.192 --> 00:23:50.457
What the hell was I thinking?

00:23:50.529 --> 00:23:52.326
A minor malfunction.

00:23:52.397 --> 00:23:54.388
Well, that's the problem.

00:23:54.466 --> 00:23:56.798
I've got a boyfriend
who malfunctions.

00:23:56.868 --> 00:23:58.563
Tom and Harry'll fix him.

00:23:58.637 --> 00:23:59.865
And if they can't?

00:23:59.938 --> 00:24:03.203
One piece of advice
you've always given me...

00:24:03.275 --> 00:24:04.367
tell the truth.

00:24:04.443 --> 00:24:07.105
Hate to break the news
to you, Michael,

00:24:07.179 --> 00:24:09.044
but I'm a starship Captain,

00:24:09.114 --> 00:24:12.174
and you're a 300 deciwatt
holodeck program.

00:24:12.250 --> 00:24:13.478
I couldn't do it.

00:24:13.552 --> 00:24:17.386
In that case...
you'll have to get creative.

00:24:19.791 --> 00:24:21.691
I'm not a
superstitious man, Doc,

00:24:21.760 --> 00:24:24.490
but I've been seeing things
I can't explain.

00:24:24.563 --> 00:24:26.224
You're not the only one.

00:24:26.298 --> 00:24:29.358
At first I thought we had
an epidemic on our hands.

00:24:29.434 --> 00:24:30.696
But this is no fever.

00:24:30.769 --> 00:24:31.929
No, it's not.

00:24:32.070 --> 00:24:33.401
Last night,
I was talking to Katie,

00:24:33.472 --> 00:24:34.439
and the next thing I knew,

00:24:34.506 --> 00:24:36.303
I was being spirited away
to God knows where.

00:24:36.374 --> 00:24:38.069
They took you? Where?

00:24:38.143 --> 00:24:40.668
Maybe it was the otherworld.
I don't know.

00:24:40.745 --> 00:24:41.939
Tom Paris was there.

00:24:42.013 --> 00:24:42.945
Young Harry, too.

00:24:43.081 --> 00:24:44.207
What did they want with you?

00:24:44.282 --> 00:24:45.249
I'm not sure.

00:24:45.317 --> 00:24:48.218
They were talking about me
like I wasn't there.

00:24:48.286 --> 00:24:50.220
Saying things about Fair Haven...

00:24:50.288 --> 00:24:52.279
that we knew too much
about them.

00:24:52.357 --> 00:24:53.289
Oh, Lord.

00:24:53.358 --> 00:24:54.382
And that's not all.

00:24:54.459 --> 00:24:55.949
They were saying something

00:24:56.094 --> 00:24:57.959
about coming to the pub tonight

00:24:58.096 --> 00:24:59.757
to change the town somehow.

00:24:59.831 --> 00:25:01.059
What does that mean?

00:25:01.132 --> 00:25:02.861
I'm damned if I know.

00:25:02.934 --> 00:25:05.027
How did you get away?

00:25:05.103 --> 00:25:07.094
With all the talk
about us knowing

00:25:07.172 --> 00:25:08.434
too much about them,

00:25:08.507 --> 00:25:12.068
I figured that if I pretended
nothing was out of the ordinary,

00:25:12.143 --> 00:25:13.371
they might let me go.

00:25:13.445 --> 00:25:16.073
And sure enough, that's
exactly what they did.

00:25:17.215 --> 00:25:20.912
I wonder if this is how
things started in Kilmanin

00:25:21.019 --> 00:25:22.577
back in '46.

00:25:22.654 --> 00:25:24.918
I'm not sure
what they have in mind.

00:25:25.056 --> 00:25:26.887
But we'd better be prepared.

00:25:41.139 --> 00:25:42.538
We're in luck.

00:25:42.607 --> 00:25:44.074
Nobody's home.

00:26:03.094 --> 00:26:05.892
If we're going to fight them,
we've got to use

00:26:06.031 --> 00:26:07.157
the right ammunition.

00:26:07.232 --> 00:26:09.427
Biddy Ramsey gave me this
before she passed on.

00:26:09.501 --> 00:26:11.799
She knew a thing or two

00:26:11.870 --> 00:26:13.701
about fairy magic
and how to fight it.

00:26:13.772 --> 00:26:14.830
Everything she learned,

00:26:14.906 --> 00:26:16.635
she learned from this book.

00:26:16.708 --> 00:26:19.302
I doubt if it says anything
about using rifles.

00:26:19.377 --> 00:26:20.844
You'd be surprised
how many things

00:26:20.912 --> 00:26:21.844
a bullet will stop.

00:26:21.913 --> 00:26:24.404
I don't like this one bit.

00:26:24.482 --> 00:26:25.710
Guns, Seamus.

00:26:25.784 --> 00:26:28.776
Under normal circumstances,
I'd agree with you, Michael...

00:26:28.853 --> 00:26:30.821
but these aren't
normal circumstances.

00:26:30.889 --> 00:26:33.153
Maybe you'd still like us
to be acting civilized.

00:26:33.224 --> 00:26:34.589
Where did that get you before?

00:26:34.659 --> 00:26:36.524
A ticket to the otherworld,
that's what.

00:26:36.595 --> 00:26:38.256
Now, we don't know
what the spirit folk

00:26:38.330 --> 00:26:39.262
have in store for us.

00:26:39.331 --> 00:26:40.389
That's just it.

00:26:40.465 --> 00:26:42.660
We don't know much
about anything, do we?

00:26:42.734 --> 00:26:43.723
And we've got Milo here

00:26:43.802 --> 00:26:45.929
acting like a holy war
has been declared.

00:26:46.071 --> 00:26:47.197
Relax, Sullivan.

00:26:47.272 --> 00:26:49.740
The rifles are only
a last resort.

00:26:49.808 --> 00:26:53.574
Besides,
we've got incantations,

00:26:53.645 --> 00:26:54.703
which are more lethal

00:26:54.779 --> 00:26:56.644
to spirit folk than any
weapons forged by man.

00:26:56.715 --> 00:26:58.410
That's how we're going

00:26:58.483 --> 00:27:00.576
to force them
back into their realm.

00:27:00.652 --> 00:27:02.085
And we've got red twine

00:27:02.153 --> 00:27:04.519
to stop them
from changing shape.

00:27:04.589 --> 00:27:06.420
And don't forget
about the ash berries.

00:27:06.491 --> 00:27:07.549
Ash berries, right.

00:27:07.626 --> 00:27:11.062
That's how you keep them
from using their powers

00:27:11.129 --> 00:27:12.790
against you.

00:27:12.864 --> 00:27:15.389
They've been walking
amongst us for weeks now,

00:27:15.467 --> 00:27:17.094
pretending to be our friends.

00:27:17.168 --> 00:27:20.194
Lying to us, making mischief.

00:27:20.271 --> 00:27:21.636
What's next?

00:27:21.706 --> 00:27:23.105
Yeah!

00:27:23.174 --> 00:27:24.732
What is next, Seamus?

00:27:24.809 --> 00:27:26.606
I for one am not going
to stand by

00:27:26.678 --> 00:27:28.942
and let them run roughshod
over our town.

00:27:30.849 --> 00:27:34.785
We can't rest until we've driven
every one of them out.

00:27:36.921 --> 00:27:38.218
They're here!

00:27:38.289 --> 00:27:42.055
Tom Paris and Harry Kim...
they're in Sullivan's right now!

00:27:42.127 --> 00:27:43.924
Who's with me?

00:27:43.995 --> 00:27:45.292
Come on.

00:27:56.007 --> 00:27:57.531
There they are!

00:27:59.611 --> 00:28:01.203
Seamus, let us explain.

00:28:01.279 --> 00:28:02.871
You're making a mistake.

00:28:02.947 --> 00:28:07.043
The only mistake we made
was letting you into Fair Haven.

00:28:07.118 --> 00:28:08.085
What's that?

00:28:08.153 --> 00:28:09.142
Shoot it.

00:28:09.220 --> 00:28:11.279
No, wait, wait. That's
a delicate piece of...

00:28:11.356 --> 00:28:12.823
...technology.

00:28:12.891 --> 00:28:16.520
Warning. Holodeck controls and
safety protocols are off-line.

00:28:16.594 --> 00:28:17.561
A voice from the otherworld.

00:28:17.629 --> 00:28:20.097
Emergency overrides
are malfunctioning.

00:28:23.068 --> 00:28:25.195
Computer, freeze program!

00:28:25.270 --> 00:28:26.999
62 percent compliance.

00:28:27.072 --> 00:28:28.903
They're getting away!

00:28:29.007 --> 00:28:29.996
After them!

00:28:36.981 --> 00:28:38.676
Computer, exit.

00:28:59.604 --> 00:29:00.935
Gunfire?

00:29:01.005 --> 00:29:02.836
Two shots directly
at the primary controls.

00:29:02.907 --> 00:29:04.204
We can't deactivate the program

00:29:04.275 --> 00:29:05.674
or get the safeties
back on line.

00:29:05.744 --> 00:29:06.870
Where are Tom and Harry now?

00:29:07.011 --> 00:29:08.478
St. Mary's.

00:29:08.546 --> 00:29:10.173
Looks like some
of your parishioners

00:29:10.248 --> 00:29:11.408
are holding them captive.

00:29:11.483 --> 00:29:13.883
We can't blame them
for being frightened.

00:29:13.985 --> 00:29:16.715
They must think we're
some kind of sorcerers.

00:29:16.788 --> 00:29:18.346
Well, we could use
a little magic right now,

00:29:18.423 --> 00:29:19.583
because that's about
the only way

00:29:19.657 --> 00:29:20.885
we're going to get
our people back.

00:29:20.959 --> 00:29:21.891
Transporters?

00:29:21.960 --> 00:29:22.892
Too many stray photons.

00:29:22.961 --> 00:29:23.893
We can't get a lock.

00:29:23.962 --> 00:29:25.327
We should enter the holodeck

00:29:25.396 --> 00:29:27.660
with a security team
and take them by force.

00:29:27.732 --> 00:29:28.664
Need I remind you

00:29:28.733 --> 00:29:30.860
that the holo-characters
have weapons as well.

00:29:30.935 --> 00:29:32.334
We'd be risking armed conflict.

00:29:32.403 --> 00:29:34.064
Well, then let's
just pull the plug.

00:29:34.139 --> 00:29:35.697
Cut power to the holo-grid.

00:29:35.774 --> 00:29:38.538
That would purge the
program from our database.

00:29:38.610 --> 00:29:39.804
Exactly.

00:29:39.878 --> 00:29:41.470
But we'd lose Fair Haven

00:29:41.546 --> 00:29:42.877
and all its people.

00:29:42.947 --> 00:29:44.710
They're not people.
They're holograms.

00:29:44.783 --> 00:29:46.876
And they weren't programmed
to be violent.

00:29:46.951 --> 00:29:48.748
I don't believe
they'd harm anyone.

00:29:48.820 --> 00:29:50.185
You can't be certain.

00:29:50.255 --> 00:29:51.313
Well, I am certain.

00:29:51.389 --> 00:29:54.017
And I'd like to find
a less drastic solution.

00:29:54.092 --> 00:29:55.923
With all due respect, Captain,

00:29:56.060 --> 00:29:57.891
Michael can be reprogrammed...

00:29:57.962 --> 00:29:59.259
Tom and Harry can't.

00:29:59.330 --> 00:30:01.059
One problem at a time,
B'Elanna.

00:30:01.132 --> 00:30:03.327
The people of Fair Haven
may not be real,

00:30:03.401 --> 00:30:05.198
but our feelings
toward them are.

00:30:05.270 --> 00:30:07.204
I won't destroy
these relationships

00:30:07.272 --> 00:30:08.739
if we can find another way.

00:30:08.807 --> 00:30:11.037
Now, if we could attach
transport enhancers

00:30:11.109 --> 00:30:12.098
onto Tom and Harry,

00:30:12.177 --> 00:30:14.907
would that be enough
to cut through the interference?

00:30:14.979 --> 00:30:16.241
I believe so.

00:30:16.314 --> 00:30:17.576
Good.

00:30:17.649 --> 00:30:19.776
Then, we just need to get
someone close to them.

00:30:19.851 --> 00:30:21.045
Doctor.

00:30:21.119 --> 00:30:24.247
A few words of inspiration
should get me within reach.

00:30:24.322 --> 00:30:26.381
I suggest you use
your mobile emitter.

00:30:26.457 --> 00:30:28.755
It'll isolate you
from the malfunctions.

00:30:28.827 --> 00:30:30.419
Good idea.

00:30:30.495 --> 00:30:32.429
Stand by to cut power
to the holo-grid,

00:30:32.497 --> 00:30:34.055
in case things get out of hand.

00:30:34.132 --> 00:30:35.156
Aye, Captain.

00:30:35.233 --> 00:30:36.325
In the meantime,

00:30:36.401 --> 00:30:38.631
I believe your flock
needs tending.

00:30:40.538 --> 00:30:44.269
Ex labis caelstium sanctorum

00:30:44.342 --> 00:30:50.110
super ad aures atorum
larvarum mentientium.

00:30:50.181 --> 00:30:52.547
From the lips
of the heavenly saints above

00:30:52.617 --> 00:30:53.948
to the ears of the dark

00:30:54.018 --> 00:30:55.713
and lying spirits...

00:30:55.787 --> 00:30:57.152
may your spectral forms

00:30:57.222 --> 00:31:01.283
be cast back to the otherworld!

00:31:05.563 --> 00:31:08.123
Back to the otherworld!

00:31:09.934 --> 00:31:13.199
There must be some trick
to these incantations.

00:31:13.271 --> 00:31:15.501
What do you make of this,
Seamus?

00:31:18.743 --> 00:31:20.335
We should just
tell them the truth.

00:31:20.411 --> 00:31:21.378
Oh, good idea, Harry.

00:31:21.446 --> 00:31:22.538
Do you want to tell them

00:31:22.614 --> 00:31:24.241
they're only holograms,
or should I?

00:31:24.315 --> 00:31:27.580
I see you left your box
of charms at Sullivan's.

00:31:27.652 --> 00:31:30.917
I believe this is the talisman
you used on Maggie.

00:31:30.989 --> 00:31:33.423
Maybe we should turn
them into cows.

00:31:34.659 --> 00:31:37.856
No, no, there's been
a misunderstanding.

00:31:37.929 --> 00:31:40.090
We are not spirit folk.

00:31:41.199 --> 00:31:43.667
I think we've heard enough
of your lies, Tommy-Boy.

00:31:43.735 --> 00:31:46.135
I say we tie them up
to the lamppost in the square

00:31:46.204 --> 00:31:47.603
and show them how spirit folk

00:31:47.672 --> 00:31:49.299
were dealt with
in the olden days.

00:31:50.408 --> 00:31:51.875
Leave them alone.

00:31:51.943 --> 00:31:53.808
It's bad enough
you've got them trussed up

00:31:53.878 --> 00:31:54.902
like Christmas turkeys.

00:31:54.979 --> 00:31:56.606
There's no need
for foolish threats!

00:31:56.681 --> 00:31:58.740
You'd better watch
whose side you're on.

00:31:58.816 --> 00:32:00.306
You're not above
suspicion yourself.

00:32:00.385 --> 00:32:01.477
Oh, so we're going to start

00:32:01.552 --> 00:32:03.213
turning on one another
now, is that it?

00:32:03.288 --> 00:32:05.552
We are if you're choosing
them over your own people.

00:32:05.623 --> 00:32:06.851
Sinners!

00:32:08.593 --> 00:32:11.027
Sinners, all of you.

00:32:11.095 --> 00:32:14.587
You have the audacity
to turn a house of worship

00:32:14.666 --> 00:32:15.655
into a prison?

00:32:15.733 --> 00:32:18.133
This is where we gather
to pledge our love

00:32:18.202 --> 00:32:19.294
for our fellow man...

00:32:19.370 --> 00:32:21.668
not condemnation.

00:32:21.739 --> 00:32:23.730
You should all be
ashamed of yourselves.

00:32:23.808 --> 00:32:26.572
Quite a rousing sermon, Father,

00:32:26.644 --> 00:32:28.703
but I'm afraid
you're not going to find

00:32:28.780 --> 00:32:30.042
too many takers tonight.

00:32:30.114 --> 00:32:32.048
Not after your
little vanishing act.

00:32:32.116 --> 00:32:34.744
It seems Grace Declan
saw you disappear

00:32:34.819 --> 00:32:36.377
into thin air on Sunday.

00:32:36.454 --> 00:32:38.945
Aye. I'd say that sounds
a wee bit unholy.

00:32:39.057 --> 00:32:41.082
You're in league
with them, aren't you?

00:32:41.159 --> 00:32:42.524
Tie him up with the others.

00:32:42.593 --> 00:32:43.719
Listen to yourselves.

00:32:43.795 --> 00:32:46.525
You're letting confusion
and fear get the best of you.

00:32:49.000 --> 00:32:50.490
Doctor to Janeway.
What's this?

00:32:50.568 --> 00:32:52.798
Fire and brimstone
don't seem to be working.

00:32:52.870 --> 00:32:55.134
Look here, Seamus!

00:32:55.206 --> 00:32:57.538
Another talisman.

00:32:57.608 --> 00:32:59.269
Quick, get it off of him!

00:33:02.914 --> 00:33:04.279
Beam him out of there.

00:33:04.349 --> 00:33:07.716
His program's been disengaged
from the mobile emitter.

00:33:07.785 --> 00:33:10.253
And what were you planning
on doing with these?

00:33:10.321 --> 00:33:13.017
Those are the devices
that will allow us to leave.

00:33:13.091 --> 00:33:16.424
Just put them on our coats
and we'll be on our way.

00:33:16.494 --> 00:33:19.395
You'd like us to believe
that, wouldn't you?

00:33:19.464 --> 00:33:21.932
Something tells me
these are spirit charms.

00:33:22.000 --> 00:33:22.932
We pin them on you

00:33:23.001 --> 00:33:24.730
and God knows
what powers you'll have.

00:33:24.802 --> 00:33:25.734
This is no charm.

00:33:25.803 --> 00:33:27.168
It's some kind of a machine.

00:33:27.238 --> 00:33:28.466
Oh, it doesn't matter.

00:33:28.539 --> 00:33:30.939
They won't be telling us
the truth about any of it.

00:33:31.075 --> 00:33:33.771
Oh, they'll tell us
the truth, all right.

00:33:33.845 --> 00:33:35.540
There's more than one way

00:33:35.613 --> 00:33:37.672
to pluck the wings
off a pixie.

00:33:37.749 --> 00:33:39.273
We'll induce a trance.

00:33:39.350 --> 00:33:42.444
Give me Billy Ramsey's spoon.

00:33:44.322 --> 00:33:48.452
Keep your eyes
on the silverware, Father.

00:33:50.495 --> 00:33:52.725
I hope you know
this is blasphemous.

00:33:53.865 --> 00:33:55.730
Oh, this is ridiculous.

00:33:55.800 --> 00:33:59.634
I can't be hypnotized.

00:34:04.876 --> 00:34:07.401
I can't isolate
the Doctor's program.

00:34:07.478 --> 00:34:10.879
He's been integrated
into the Fair Haven matrix.

00:34:10.948 --> 00:34:12.472
He's vulnerable.

00:34:12.550 --> 00:34:14.177
Tell us the words.

00:34:14.252 --> 00:34:19.157
How do we banish the spirit folk
to the otherworld?

00:34:19.223 --> 00:34:22.283
There is no otherworld.

00:34:22.360 --> 00:34:24.453
Only Voyager.

00:34:24.529 --> 00:34:25.427
"Voyager"?

00:34:25.496 --> 00:34:28.590
Federation Starship,
Intrepid-class,

00:34:28.666 --> 00:34:32.033
registry number NCC-74656.

00:34:32.103 --> 00:34:34.264
You're from some sort of vessel?

00:34:34.338 --> 00:34:35.498
Mm-hmm.

00:34:35.573 --> 00:34:37.473
He's talking gibberish.

00:34:37.542 --> 00:34:38.702
Maybe he's not.

00:34:38.776 --> 00:34:39.902
This ship of yours...

00:34:39.977 --> 00:34:41.467
is that where you took me?

00:34:41.546 --> 00:34:42.877
Look here.

00:34:42.947 --> 00:34:46.713
It says if you can get a spirit
to reveal his true name,

00:34:46.784 --> 00:34:50.220
you'll render yourself
impervious to his charms.

00:34:51.422 --> 00:34:52.946
What is your true name?

00:34:53.024 --> 00:34:55.618
I haven't decided on one yet.

00:34:56.861 --> 00:34:58.419
Katie's on that ship, isn't she?

00:34:58.496 --> 00:34:59.724
Tell me where to find her,

00:34:59.797 --> 00:35:01.560
so I can get
to the bottom of this.

00:35:01.632 --> 00:35:03.395
I'm sorry, Michael.
I can't do that.

00:35:03.468 --> 00:35:06.335
Then I guess I'm asking
the wrong person.

00:35:10.374 --> 00:35:12.467
Tell me how to get to Voyager.

00:35:12.543 --> 00:35:13.567
No, Michael.

00:35:13.644 --> 00:35:14.668
What are you doing, Sullivan?

00:35:14.745 --> 00:35:15.905
No man who's gone
to the otherworld

00:35:16.047 --> 00:35:16.911
has ever come back.

00:35:17.048 --> 00:35:18.743
I have.

00:35:21.552 --> 00:35:23.543
How do I get there?

00:35:30.761 --> 00:35:34.629
Captain, the mobile emitter
has been activated.

00:35:34.699 --> 00:35:36.633
Lock on to the signal.

00:35:48.479 --> 00:35:50.037
I had a cousin, went to America.

00:35:50.114 --> 00:35:52.480
He saw some strange things...

00:35:52.550 --> 00:35:53.676
but nothing like this.

00:35:55.753 --> 00:35:58.051
You're not in America.

00:36:02.393 --> 00:36:04.384
This is a starship.

00:36:04.462 --> 00:36:05.451
It's called...

00:36:05.530 --> 00:36:07.794
Voyager.

00:36:07.865 --> 00:36:09.890
I know.

00:36:10.034 --> 00:36:13.026
Should I transfer him
back to the holodeck?

00:36:13.104 --> 00:36:15.163
No.

00:36:20.645 --> 00:36:23.637
My name isn't Katie O'Clare.

00:36:23.714 --> 00:36:26.547
It's Kathryn Janeway.

00:36:26.617 --> 00:36:28.744
Captain Kathryn Janeway.

00:36:28.819 --> 00:36:32.186
It's going to be difficult
to explain.

00:36:32.256 --> 00:36:34.884
Maybe it's best
if I just show you.

00:36:34.959 --> 00:36:36.517
You've seen my world.

00:36:36.594 --> 00:36:41.190
It seems fair that I should
have a chance to see yours.

00:36:41.265 --> 00:36:42.391
Captain?

00:36:56.681 --> 00:36:59.616
So this really is a starship.

00:36:59.684 --> 00:37:02.016
But how can that be possible?

00:37:02.086 --> 00:37:05.249
Have you ever read
The Time Machine

00:37:05.323 --> 00:37:06.620
by H. G. Wells?

00:37:06.691 --> 00:37:09.888
Are you telling me you've
traveled back through time?

00:37:09.994 --> 00:37:11.894
In a manner of speaking.

00:37:17.835 --> 00:37:20.133
That's Patrick Gibson
and his cousin, Frank.

00:37:20.204 --> 00:37:21.432
They work on the wharf.

00:37:21.505 --> 00:37:23.496
They're in my pub
every Saturday night

00:37:23.574 --> 00:37:24.836
for the rings tournament.

00:37:24.909 --> 00:37:28.106
You'll find a lot
of familiar faces on Voyager.

00:37:28.179 --> 00:37:30.170
We've been visiting
your town for months.

00:37:30.248 --> 00:37:31.408
Why?

00:37:34.151 --> 00:37:36.517
We're explorers.

00:37:36.587 --> 00:37:39.181
I-I'm not letting you
off the hook that easy,

00:37:39.257 --> 00:37:40.224
Kathryn Janeway.

00:37:40.291 --> 00:37:42.282
There must be more
interesting places

00:37:42.360 --> 00:37:43.793
to explore than Fair Haven.

00:37:43.861 --> 00:37:46.091
Can you think of any place
you'd rather be?

00:37:46.163 --> 00:37:49.132
I guess that leaves
but one question.

00:37:49.200 --> 00:37:52.795
How long are you
planning on staying?

00:37:52.870 --> 00:37:54.269
Well, that depends...

00:37:54.338 --> 00:37:56.465
if we're still welcome.

00:37:56.540 --> 00:37:58.508
My door is always open.

00:37:59.910 --> 00:38:01.810
But you're the Captain
of a starship.

00:38:01.879 --> 00:38:03.244
I'm a barkeep.

00:38:05.516 --> 00:38:07.780
Just because we're
from different worlds

00:38:07.852 --> 00:38:10.286
doesn't mean we can't
care for each other.

00:38:10.354 --> 00:38:11.753
Tuvok to Janeway.

00:38:11.822 --> 00:38:12.880
Go ahead.

00:38:12.990 --> 00:38:15.322
The situation has escalated.

00:38:15.393 --> 00:38:18.328
The townspeople have started
to pile kindling

00:38:18.396 --> 00:38:21.695
at the base of three lampposts
in the village square.

00:38:21.766 --> 00:38:23.131
Stand by.

00:38:27.905 --> 00:38:28.894
Take them outside now!

00:38:33.144 --> 00:38:34.202
Stay back!

00:38:34.278 --> 00:38:35.870
What are you doing?

00:38:35.946 --> 00:38:38.039
That might not be
Michael Sullivan.

00:38:38.115 --> 00:38:40.208
A demon could have
taken his shape!

00:38:40.284 --> 00:38:41.546
It's me, Milo.

00:38:41.619 --> 00:38:43.883
I've been to the place
they call Voyager,

00:38:43.954 --> 00:38:45.444
and I've brought
Katie back with me.

00:38:45.523 --> 00:38:47.013
I've come in hopes that we

00:38:47.091 --> 00:38:49.423
can resolve our differences
peacefully.

00:38:49.493 --> 00:38:50.425
Peacefully?

00:38:50.494 --> 00:38:52.086
I'm sure that's what
you told the people

00:38:52.163 --> 00:38:53.187
of Kilmanin.

00:38:53.264 --> 00:38:55.664
They had nothing to do
with what happened in Kilmanin,

00:38:55.733 --> 00:38:56.927
if it happened at all.

00:38:57.068 --> 00:38:59.400
We're truly sorry
for what's happened here.

00:38:59.470 --> 00:39:01.301
We never meant
to frighten anyone.

00:39:01.372 --> 00:39:03.670
Seamus, you were right
about one thing.

00:39:03.741 --> 00:39:05.106
They are quite different
from us.

00:39:05.176 --> 00:39:07.508
But not in so many ways
that we can't be friends.

00:39:07.578 --> 00:39:10.513
And we're not spirit folk.

00:39:10.581 --> 00:39:13.846
Some of our technology
may seem like magic,

00:39:13.918 --> 00:39:16.011
but I assure you it's not.

00:39:16.087 --> 00:39:17.145
Katie showed me things

00:39:17.221 --> 00:39:18.882
that are beyond
our comprehension.

00:39:19.023 --> 00:39:21.787
They have machines that
I can't begin to describe,

00:39:21.859 --> 00:39:24.726
but not once have they
used them against us.

00:39:24.795 --> 00:39:26.820
Quite the opposite, in fact.

00:39:26.897 --> 00:39:30.094
Milo, you said you
didn't like the rain.

00:39:30.167 --> 00:39:33.261
And young Harry Kim
made it go away.

00:39:33.337 --> 00:39:35.396
Edith Mulcahey herself

00:39:35.473 --> 00:39:37.407
said that Katie O'Clare
pulled her daughter

00:39:37.475 --> 00:39:39.033
from the well
and out of harm's way.

00:39:39.110 --> 00:39:42.238
These are not the deeds
of spirits and mischief-makers.

00:39:42.313 --> 00:39:44.076
But they turned Maggie
into a cow!

00:39:44.148 --> 00:39:46.309
Uh, that'll never happen again.

00:39:46.384 --> 00:39:47.544
I swear.

00:39:47.618 --> 00:39:49.279
There, you see...

00:39:49.353 --> 00:39:50.718
we have his word.

00:39:50.788 --> 00:39:53.313
And if we can't trust
a man's word in Fair Haven,

00:39:53.391 --> 00:39:55.325
what can we trust?

00:39:55.393 --> 00:39:57.327
If you want...

00:39:57.395 --> 00:40:00.091
we'll leave
and never bother you again.

00:40:01.899 --> 00:40:05.767
But we'd prefer to find some way
to keep our friendship alive.

00:40:15.980 --> 00:40:18.210
Captain?

00:40:21.085 --> 00:40:23.451
I, for one, would like
to keep playing rings

00:40:23.521 --> 00:40:24.818
with Harry and Tom.

00:40:24.889 --> 00:40:27.790
Keep having Neelix's
steamed cabbage

00:40:27.858 --> 00:40:29.792
at The Ox and Lamb,

00:40:29.860 --> 00:40:32.021
keep taking walks with Katie.

00:40:33.364 --> 00:40:35.764
Let's not turn our backs
on these good people.

00:40:35.833 --> 00:40:38.028
If we do,
we're only punishing ourselves.

00:40:40.471 --> 00:40:43.565
Just because we're
from different worlds,

00:40:43.641 --> 00:40:46.974
doesn't mean we can't
care for each other.

00:40:53.617 --> 00:40:55.107
If we're going
to retain the program,

00:40:55.186 --> 00:40:56.983
we can't keep running
it around the clock.

00:40:57.054 --> 00:40:59.113
Not until we've repaired
these damaged systems.

00:40:59.190 --> 00:41:00.555
Not ever!

00:41:00.624 --> 00:41:02.387
We've pushed the limits
of holo-technology

00:41:02.460 --> 00:41:03.722
and they've pushed back.

00:41:03.794 --> 00:41:05.785
If we try it again,
we're just asking for trouble.

00:41:05.863 --> 00:41:08.195
I'm afraid we're
going to have to close

00:41:08.265 --> 00:41:09.664
your open door policy.

00:41:09.733 --> 00:41:11.667
Yes, ma'am.

00:41:11.735 --> 00:41:13.635
What about the characters?

00:41:13.704 --> 00:41:15.831
Should I purge their memories
of the last few days?

00:41:15.906 --> 00:41:17.669
No. Leave them.

00:41:17.741 --> 00:41:20.471
They think we're space men
from the future.

00:41:20.544 --> 00:41:22.774
It won't exactly
be like old times.

00:41:22.847 --> 00:41:25.907
We learn to accept alien species
with new technologies.

00:41:25.983 --> 00:41:27.883
Let's hope
the people of Fair Haven

00:41:27.952 --> 00:41:29.283
will learn to accept us.

00:41:29.353 --> 00:41:32.379
But before we shut down
the program and begin repairs,

00:41:32.456 --> 00:41:35.220
what do you say
to one more round at Sullivan's?

00:41:35.292 --> 00:41:37.226
My treat.

00:41:39.296 --> 00:41:41.093
You may be from the moon,
Tommy-Boy,

00:41:41.165 --> 00:41:42.564
but I've got three shillings

00:41:42.633 --> 00:41:44.726
that says I can still
whip you at rings.

00:41:44.802 --> 00:41:45.894
Ho! You're on.

00:41:45.970 --> 00:41:47.403
Hello, Harry.

00:41:47.471 --> 00:41:48.870
I'll catch up with you later.

00:41:48.939 --> 00:41:50.839
Tommy-Boy!

00:41:50.908 --> 00:41:53.706
I've been told there's a pot
of gold somewhere in Glen Abbey.

00:41:53.777 --> 00:41:54.971
Would you be able to find it

00:41:55.112 --> 00:41:56.977
with one of those fancy machines
of yours?

00:41:57.081 --> 00:41:58.776
Just follow the rainbow,
Seamus.

00:41:58.849 --> 00:41:59.975
Rainbow, eh?

00:42:00.084 --> 00:42:02.143
I didn't think you'd ever
speak to me again.

00:42:02.219 --> 00:42:04.278
Well, I suppose I can
forgive and forget.

00:42:04.355 --> 00:42:05.720
It's not that often you meet

00:42:05.789 --> 00:42:07.723
a handsome young man
from outer space.

00:42:13.297 --> 00:42:14.855
What's this,
Christmas come early?

00:42:14.932 --> 00:42:15.921
It has for you.

00:42:16.000 --> 00:42:17.160
Open it.

00:42:22.840 --> 00:42:24.671
A Connecticut Yankee
in King Arthur's Court

00:42:24.742 --> 00:42:26.710
by Mark Twain.

00:42:26.777 --> 00:42:29.678
I've read some of his books,
but not this one.

00:42:29.747 --> 00:42:33.114
It's about two people
from different times

00:42:33.183 --> 00:42:36.152
who fall in love,
among other things.

00:42:36.220 --> 00:42:37.346
Sounds familiar.

00:42:37.421 --> 00:42:38.979
Maybe I have read it.

00:42:40.057 --> 00:42:41.456
Thank you, Katie.

00:42:41.525 --> 00:42:43.390
Or Kathryn now, is it?

00:42:43.460 --> 00:42:44.825
Oh!

00:42:44.895 --> 00:42:47.295
They say if you know
a spirit's true name,

00:42:47.364 --> 00:42:52.063
that it renders you impervious
against her charms.

00:42:52.136 --> 00:42:53.626
Do you think there's
any truth in that?

00:42:53.704 --> 00:42:54.864
Not a word.

00:42:54.939 --> 00:42:56.372
I'm glad to hear it.
